# Locale Codes

To adapt content for someone, you first have to name who they are, and a **locale** is that name. At a minimum it identifies a **language** and usually a **region**.

## The Shape of a Code

A locale code can be just a language:

```
en      English
ar      Arabic
es      Spanish
fr      French
hi      Hindi
zh      Chinese
```

Or a language and a region, joined with a hyphen:

```
en-US   English as used in the United States
en-CA   English as used in Canada
es-ES   Spanish as used in Spain
es-MX   Spanish as used in Mexico
pt-BR   Portuguese as used in Brazil
pt-PT   Portuguese as used in Portugal
```

Those pairs matter. `es-ES` and `es-MX` are the same language and differ in vocabulary, currency, and number formatting; `pt-BR` and `pt-PT` differ enough that translators treat them separately.

There is also an optional **script** subtag, for a language written in more than one script:

```
fr-Brai   French written in Braille
zh-Hans   Chinese written in simplified characters
zh-Hant   Chinese written in traditional characters
```

## The Standards Behind the Codes

The codes are not invented per project.

- **Language codes** come from **ISO 639**, a long list in which French is `fr`, German is `de`, and English is `en`.
- **Region codes** come from the ISO country list, in which the United States is `US`.

By convention the language is lowercase and the region uppercase, `en-US` rather than `EN-us`, and code that compares locale codes should not assume the case is right.

## Declaring the Document's Locale

In HTML, the locale goes on the `<html>` element, in the **`lang`** attribute:

```html
<html lang="en">
```

```html
<html lang="es-MX">
```

That one attribute does a surprising amount of work:

- If the visitor's own locale differs, the **browser can offer to translate** the page.
- **Screen readers** use it to choose a pronunciation. The same letters are read very differently in English and Spanish, and getting this wrong makes a page unusable rather than merely awkward, which the accessibility lesson will come back to.
- Search engines use it to decide who to show the page to.

It is one attribute, it is easy to leave out, and leaving it out is one of the more common accessibility faults on the web.

## Reading the User's Locale

The browser knows what the user asked for:

```{javascript autorun}
console.log('preferred locale: ' + navigator.language);
console.log('all preferences:  ' + navigator.languages);
```

**`navigator.language`** is a single locale code, the user's first preference. **`navigator.languages`** is an **array** of their preferences in order, because people who read several languages can say so. `navigator.language` is normally just the first item of that list.

Preferring the list is the better habit: if you have no translation for someone's first choice, their second may still be better than your default.

## Why the Region Half Matters

Language alone is often not enough, and dates are the clearest case:

```{javascript autorun}
const date = new Date('2026-05-21T12:00:00Z');

console.log('en-US: ' + new Intl.DateTimeFormat('en-US').format(date));
console.log('en-GB: ' + new Intl.DateTimeFormat('en-GB').format(date));
console.log('ja-JP: ' + new Intl.DateTimeFormat('ja-JP').format(date));
```

`en-US` and `en-GB` are the same language, and one writes the month first while the other writes the day first. `5/21/2026` and `21/05/2026` are each unambiguous, and `5/6/2026` is not, which is how a delivery date becomes a support ticket.

`Intl.DateTimeFormat` is a later reading's subject. What matters here is that it takes a **locale code**, which is why getting the code right comes first.

## Exercises

1. Write `parseLocale`, which splits a locale code into its parts.

    ```{javascript}
    function parseLocale(code) {
      // TODO: return an object with a language and a region.
      // 'es-MX' gives { language: 'es', region: 'MX' }.
      // A code with no region gives a region of null.
    }

    console.log(parseLocale('es-MX'));
    console.log(parseLocale('en'));
    ```
    ```+solution
    function parseLocale(code) {
      const parts = code.split('-');

      if (parts.length > 1) {
        return { language: parts[0], region: parts[1] };
      }

      return { language: parts[0], region: null };
    }

    console.log(parseLocale('es-MX'));
    console.log(parseLocale('en'));
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(parseLocale('es-MX')).should('equal', { language: 'es', region: 'MX' }).run('Split the code at the hyphen: the language comes first, the region second', 'Split a language and region');
    wrap(parseLocale('pt-BR').region).should('equal', 'BR').run('It should work for any code, so `pt-BR` gives a region of `BR`', 'Works for other codes');
    wrap(parseLocale('en')).should('equal', { language: 'en', region: null }).run('A code with no region should give a `region` of `null`', 'Handled a language-only code');
    ```

2. Declare this document's locale as Spanish as used in Mexico.

    ```{html}
    <html>
      <head>
        <title>Mi sitio</title>
      </head>
      <body>
        <h1>Hola</h1>
      </body>
    </html>
    ```
    ```+solution
    <html lang="es-MX">
      <head>
        <title>Mi sitio</title>
      </head>
      <body>
        <h1>Hola</h1>
      </body>
    </html>
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(/<html[^>]*\slang\s*=\s*["']es-MX["']/.test(source)).should('equal', true).run('Add `lang="es-MX"` to the `html` element', 'Declared the locale');
    wrap(/lang\s*=\s*["']es["']/.test(source)).should('equal', false).run('Include the region as well as the language, since `es` alone does not say Mexico', 'Included the region');
    ```

3. You are building a site for Spanish-speaking users in Mexico. What is the most precise way to declare that?

    ```{mcq}
    Which line identifies the document's locale?
    correct: Correct! The `lang` attribute on the `html` element is where a document's locale goes, and `es-MX` names both the language and the region.
    wrong: One of these is on the right element, with the right attribute, and the right code.
    [x] `<html lang="es-MX">`
    [ ] `<meta charset="es-MX">`
    [ ] `<body locale="spanish-mexico">`
    [ ] `<html lang="en-ES">`
    ```

4. Which statement about the `navigator` object is right?

    ```{mcq}
    How do the two properties relate?
    correct: Correct! `navigator.languages` is the ordered array of preferences, and `navigator.language` is normally the first entry in it.
    wrong: One is singular and one is plural, and that is the whole difference.
    [x] `navigator.languages` is an array of preferred locales; `navigator.language` is typically its first item
    [ ] `navigator.language` lists every language the user has ever used
    [ ] A meta tag is needed before `navigator.language` works
    [ ] `navigator.language` gives only the country code, never the language
    ```

